<header class="story-header"><h1>Our Story</h1><p>A church begins with people. At KCMC, the story is one of gathering, welcoming newcomers and finding ways to serve a changing community.</p></header>
<article class="story-sheet">
<section><h2>A congregation before a sanctuary</h2><p>According to Fern Sherer’s account, the Sherer family built the Hillbilly Bowl as the Table Rock Lake community developed. The Joplin District of the Methodist Church purchased a church lot for $5,000 and asked retired pastor John Wesley McKinney to begin organizing a congregation.</p><p>The Hillbilly Bowl opened on August 17, 1962. The first church services were held there the following week. There was no sanctuary yet—just people gathering to worship. Twenty-two members were accepted on December 9.</p><p>In January 1963, Lee Whiteside was assigned as pastor and helped oversee groundbreaking, consecration and construction. Dick and Fern Sherer joined in March. The congregation’s official charter was presented on June 12, 1963, when it had 53 members.</p></section>
<section><h2>Growing with the community</h2><p>The sanctuary expanded in 1972. Fern recorded membership at 750 by 1976. Another 8,700 square feet of offices and Sunday school rooms were added in 1982; when that addition was dedicated on Palm Sunday, membership stood at 864. Later expansion helped create the building we know today.</p><p>The church’s contribution extended beyond its building. Fern recalled community activities including the Tri-Lakes Chorale, Well Baby Clinic, Hughes Memorial Band, Kimberling Klub, Red Cross Bloodmobile, driver’s-license testing, Hospice, Young Life and County Extension Homemakers, alongside worship, study, fellowship and mission work.</p><p>These are activities recalled in the historical account; they are not a list of programs currently offered. <a href="./#events">See upcoming gatherings</a> for today’s published events.</p></section>
<section><h2>People who helped build the church</h2><p>Women were central from the earliest days. Virginia “Mom” Sherer helped establish the Women’s Society of Christian Service. When the church needed an organ, Ruth Waltz and Vera Lou Biggerstaff co-signed the note, Ray Gossett paid the interest, and the women raised funds through bake sales, dinners and other projects.</p><p>In later years, the women provided handbells and supported missions and local charities. Their work is part of a larger story of people sharing their gifts to help their neighbors.</p></section>
<section><h2>A welcome that keeps beginning</h2><p>Fern observed, “Our congregation seems to always be changing.” People came to the lake, found fellowship and brought new needs and ideas. She also remembered Pastor Leland Loy reminding the congregation that a new church needed new ideas.</p><p>The supplied church-history summary carries that invitation forward: a place where people are welcomed, Scripture is opened, gifts are discovered and neighbors serve neighbors.</p><blockquote><p>“Perhaps we can explore new ways of doing things.”</p></blockquote><p>Whether you are looking for a church home or a way to become more involved, you are invited to take your next step with KCMC.</p></section>
<footer class="story-source"><p>Adapted from the three-page <em>Summary of Our Church History</em> supplied to KCMC Connect. That summary credits its core historical account to Fern Sherer, November 18, 2023. Dates, membership figures and recollections above are attributed to that account.</p></footer>
